			<div class="content" id="post-content-2570081">
									

<blockquote>
<p>This is some sort of bacterium,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't have any idea why you think that is the case, it doesn't have any of the taxonomic characteristics of Monera.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The reason there are insects that look like sticks but no horses that look like sticks is because insects breed very very fast and there's very very many of them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Probably not, mimicry isn't compatible with the successful equine adaptations and would be selected against. There are definitely vertebrate mimics!<br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatfish">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatfish</a><br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leafy_seadragon">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leafy_seadragon</a><br />
<a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingsnake">https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingsnake</a></p>
<p>That said, yes this is way too fast. It is also an anomaly. Being supernaturally fast doesn't also mean that it is necessarily supernaturally smart.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>You know, probably not. People will drink store-brand soda, but they will not drink obviously counterfeit shit.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You seem to think that everyone pays very close attention to the details in their environment at all times. I disagree, and I think that there are several studies of human perception that back this up. But this is one of those things that it is very hard to convince people of (perhaps because of the natural desire to believe that one's own perception is inviable), so whatever.</p>

							</div>

								
